1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,252
Yes.

2
00:00:00,252 --> 00:00:10,561
Your short track blade ends have to be rounded, and by the end of this guide you will know how to check all four of them in two minutes and how to fix one at home.

3
00:00:10,561 --> 00:00:16,062
We will cover what the rule actually says, why it exists, and what a closed tube means.

4
00:00:16,062 --> 00:00:27,066
Then we will gauge a tip against a simple paper circle, shape it without touching the part of the blade you skate on, and look at the moments when it is worth checking again.

5
00:00:27,766 --> 00:00:31,798
The requirement comes from the International Skating Union.

6
00:00:31,798 --> 00:00:40,749
Its communication on protection equipment in speed skating and short track is based on Rules 224 and 291, and the wording is exact.

7
00:00:40,749 --> 00:00:49,837
The blades of all skaters must have the tubes closed, and the blade ends must be rounded off with a minimum radius of 10 millimetres.

8
00:00:49,837 --> 00:00:53,732
National bodies publish the same idea in their own words.

9
00:00:53,732 --> 00:01:00,497
Skate Canada, for example, says blade tubes must be closed and front and back tips must be rounded.

10
00:01:00,497 --> 00:01:08,424
So read the regulations for the competition you have entered, and check there is nothing newer before a major event.

11
00:01:08,424 --> 00:01:10,405
The principle stays the same.

12
00:01:10,405 --> 00:01:14,710
No points at either end of either blade, and no open tube ends.

13
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:17,705
Think about what short track is.

14
00:01:17,705 --> 00:01:23,297
A pack of skaters races on a 111.12 metre oval with sharpened steel underfoot.

15
00:01:23,297 --> 00:01:28,747
When somebody goes down, everyone arrives at the same patch of ice at speed.

16
00:01:28,747 --> 00:01:34,913
In that moment, the ends of a blade are the parts most likely to reach another skater.

17
00:01:34,913 --> 00:01:39,861
They sit furthest forward and furthest back, so they get there first.

18
00:01:39,861 --> 00:01:42,370
The rule itself gives no reasoning.

19
00:01:42,370 --> 00:01:44,234
But it is not hard to see.

20
00:01:44,234 --> 00:01:48,106
A square corner concentrates that contact in one spot.

21
00:01:48,106 --> 00:01:49,755
A curve spreads it out.

22
00:01:50,455 --> 00:01:54,258
The rule gives you a radius, but not a way to eyeball one.

23
00:01:54,258 --> 00:01:56,815
In practice, you judge the end profile.

24
00:01:56,815 --> 00:02:01,076
That is the shape you see when you look at the tip from the side.

25
00:02:01,076 --> 00:02:03,305
Put cut resistant gloves on first.

26
00:02:03,305 --> 00:02:07,107
The tip is blunt, but the edges either side of it are not.

27
00:02:07,107 --> 00:02:08,484
Now look at each end.

28
00:02:08,484 --> 00:02:16,089
The steel should finish as a continuous curve, not as a corner where the running surface meets the end of the blade.

29
00:02:16,089 --> 00:02:17,728
Then check all four ends.

30
00:02:17,728 --> 00:02:19,760
Front and back, left and right.

31
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:25,136
A tip that met a kerb, a bench corner or another skate is usually the odd one out.

32
00:02:25,136 --> 00:02:29,856
While you are there, feel for a raised lip along the sides near the tip.

33
00:02:29,856 --> 00:02:35,559
That is burr from an earlier sharpen, not a shape problem, and it comes off in seconds.

34
00:02:36,259 --> 00:02:39,229
Here is the end profile, seen from the side.

35
00:02:39,229 --> 00:02:42,806
On the left, the steel stops dead and leaves a point.

36
00:02:42,806 --> 00:02:46,518
On the right, the end finishes as one continuous curve.

37
00:02:46,518 --> 00:02:54,684
To test yours, use a circle 20 millimetres across, because a 10 millimetre radius is the curve of a 20 millimetre circle.

38
00:02:54,684 --> 00:02:56,844
Hold the circle against the tip.

39
00:02:56,844 --> 00:03:03,255
If the blade curves tighter than the circle's edge, that end is under radius and needs shaping.

40
00:03:03,255 --> 00:03:08,722
A coin of about that size works for a quick look, but coin sizes vary by country.

41
00:03:08,722 --> 00:03:12,772
A 20 millimetre circle printed on paper is the honest gauge.

42
00:03:13,471 --> 00:03:18,604
The tube is the spine that carries the running steel and bolts into your cups.

43
00:03:18,604 --> 00:03:21,038
The rule only says it must be closed.

44
00:03:21,038 --> 00:03:31,236
In practice, that means the ends are capped rather than left open, so there is no open edge at the front or back to catch a hand, a suit or another skater.

45
00:03:31,236 --> 00:03:34,132
Short track race blades come built that way.

46
00:03:34,132 --> 00:03:41,106
The most common way a compliant blade stops being one is a tube end split or peeled open in a heavy crash.

47
00:03:41,106 --> 00:03:48,343
So after a fall, treat the tube ends as part of your inspection, alongside the cups, the bolts and the rocker.

48
00:03:49,043 --> 00:03:54,795
If a tip has picked up a corner, you can fix it at home with the same kit you sharpen with.

49
00:03:54,795 --> 00:03:59,661
You need something to hold the blade still, a bench stone and a deburr stone.

50
00:03:59,661 --> 00:04:02,821
Clamp the blade in a jig so the steel cannot move.

51
00:04:02,821 --> 00:04:06,486
Then work the tip with the coarse side of the bench stone.

52
00:04:06,486 --> 00:04:11,984
Use short strokes that follow the curve you want, rather than scrubbing back and forth.

53
00:04:11,984 --> 00:04:16,408
Check the profile against your 20 millimetre circle every few strokes.

54
00:04:16,408 --> 00:04:18,620
Taking a little more steel is easy.

55
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:20,137
Putting any back is not.

56
00:04:20,837 --> 00:04:23,023
One rule keeps you out of trouble.

57
00:04:23,023 --> 00:04:26,173
Shape the end profile, never the running surface.

58
00:04:26,173 --> 00:04:30,802
In this diagram, the green zones at each end are the only parts in play.

59
00:04:30,802 --> 00:04:34,273
The blue section in the middle is the running surface.

60
00:04:34,273 --> 00:04:37,681
Your rocker, your bend and your edges all live there.

61
00:04:37,681 --> 00:04:44,560
The last centimetre or so at each end never carries you, so shaping it changes nothing about how you skate.

62
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:49,574
Take a stone to the middle, though, and you have changed how the blade skates.

63
00:04:50,274 --> 00:04:59,697
Once the curve is right, blend the shoulders where the new curve meets the sides of the blade, so no flat spot or fresh corner is left behind.

64
00:04:59,697 --> 00:05:06,333
Finish with a few light passes of a deburr stone so the tip carries no lip, then wipe the blade dry.

65
00:05:06,333 --> 00:05:11,177
Skate a session on it before you race, as you would after any sharpening.

66
00:05:11,177 --> 00:05:11,973
One warning.

67
00:05:11,973 --> 00:05:17,282
If a tip is bent, cracked or missing material, that is a different conversation.

68
00:05:17,282 --> 00:05:21,861
A blade in that state belongs in front of your coach, not on a stone.

69
00:05:22,561 --> 00:05:24,322
Four moments deserve a look.

70
00:05:24,322 --> 00:05:28,223
After any crash where a blade met another skate or the boards.

71
00:05:28,223 --> 00:05:37,408
After heavy work with a coarse stone, because removing material along the length can leave a sharper transition at the ends than you started with.

72
00:05:37,408 --> 00:05:40,553
After travel, because blades knock about in a bag.

73
00:05:40,553 --> 00:05:44,894
And at the start of a season, when a set has been sitting for months.

74
00:05:44,894 --> 00:05:47,536
New blades are worth the same two minutes.

75
00:05:47,536 --> 00:05:52,066
A race blade normally arrives with the ends shaped and the tubes closed.

76
00:05:52,066 --> 00:05:57,665
But a set that has been through a courier is still a set you have not inspected yourself.

77
00:05:58,365 --> 00:06:01,029
Does rounding the ends make you slower?

78
00:06:01,029 --> 00:06:01,234
No.

79
00:06:01,234 --> 00:06:08,542
The ends are not part of the surface that carries you, so shaping them changes nothing about grip or glide.

80
00:06:08,542 --> 00:06:11,479
Does the same rule cover long track blades?

81
00:06:11,479 --> 00:06:11,753
Yes.

82
00:06:11,753 --> 00:06:19,334
The ISU requirement covers both speed skating and short track, and it is written for the blades of all skaters.

83
00:06:19,334 --> 00:06:21,930
And what if your ends are not rounded?

84
00:06:21,930 --> 00:06:26,233
Then it is non compliant equipment, not a matter of preference.

85
00:06:26,233 --> 00:06:30,673
What that means on the day belongs to the officials at your meet.

86
00:06:30,673 --> 00:06:35,728
A blade you shaped at home is a blade you never have to discuss with them.

87
00:06:36,428 --> 00:06:38,886
So here is the one thing to remember.

88
00:06:38,886 --> 00:06:43,403
Four ends, a 20 millimetre circle, two minutes at the kitchen table.

89
00:06:43,403 --> 00:06:47,057
The reward for doing it is that nothing happens at all.

90
00:06:47,057 --> 00:06:55,428
The full guide is on the LiveLica blog, with links to our guides on sharpening, deburring and checking your kit after a crash.

91
00:06:55,428 --> 00:06:57,488
The link is in the description.
